<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Balkans</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Balkan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Balkans are the peninsula of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> between the Adriatic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> and Black seas. The region was a core part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and later of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, which ruled it for centuries. Nationalist movements and great-power rivalry made the Balkans the scene of Ottoman decline and of the crisis that drew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/balkans.jpg" alt="Balkans" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ikonact · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Geographic_map_of_Balkan_Peninsula.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
